[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says "one issue, one fix queued". Its reports describe three problems.
Each needs its own fix, and each now has its own ticket. The fix queued for the parser closes only
the first one.

<Callout kind="warn">
Do not close the hint or one-day reports when the parser fix ships. They will still happen.
</Callout>

## The split

<SplitTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

The ticket export has more rows than reports: its pages overlap, so some reports come twice. The
counts here are per report id.

## The three problems

**`parser`: the shared parser reads every date month first.** A tenant that writes the day first
(en-GB, en-IE, de-DE, fr-FR, nl-NL and the rest on file) gets day and month swapped: 4 March becomes
3 April. It comes in through the app, by email, and through `booking-v2` forms. This is the bug
#4471 was named for.

**`hint`: the `booking-v3` form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field.** The tenant's people type
month first because the hint tells them to, and the form reads the date the tenant's way, day first.
The result looks the same as `parser` (a swap), so subject-line dedupe merged it. Fixing the parser
does not fix it; the hint and the form have to agree. Only day-first tenants on `booking-v3`, only
through the form.

**`one-day`: the invite lands one day off.** No month and day swap at all: the date moves by one
day, or differs between people in different places ("Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in
Toronto got Monday."). That points at time zone handling when the invite is made, not at reading
the date. The desk already noted these (`desk-notes.json`), but the #4471 table counts them under
`parser` and only footnotes them.

## What the #4471 page shows instead

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

It finds the `hint` rows and marks them "no ticket of its own", and it hides the one-day reports in
a footnote. The page above it still says one issue.

## Why it happened

- [Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject line alone. Every
  problem here produces the same subjects ("Wrong day on the invite"), so subject dedupe cannot tell
  them apart. The bodies and the tenant can.
- Intake's tag is a guess from the reporter's browser, not from the tenant. The "intake tagged"
  column shows how often it disagrees with the cause. Don't triage on it.
- The dedupe pages were last reviewed in 2024 and show as stale.

## For the desk

1. File new date reports against the problem in the table, not against #4471.
2. Read the tenant (locale, booking form) and the body before merging. A one-day move is not a swap.
3. Decision 0001 needs a successor record that merges on cause, not subject.

